# ACOS Nemesis Protocol

Thin by design (D-24): the rules live in the engine, the hooks and the judge's charter, not
in this file. Everything below routes to the CLI:

```bash
N="/Users/zee/.bun/bin/bun /Users/zee/Documents/Vibe Coding/ACOS 3.0/.claude/skills/acos-nemesis-protocol/scripts/nemesis.ts"
```

PRD: `prd/PRD.md` · decisions: `prd/DECISIONS.md` · trace: `prd/TRACE.json` · rulebook:
`rulebook/SECURITY-PROTOCOL.md` (byte copy of the portal's) → `rulebook/rulebook.json` (generated).

## Routing (what to do when invoked)

| Invocation | Do |
|---|---|
| `/acos-nemesis-protocol enroll [dir] [--prd <file>]` | `$N enroll …` — installs the four hooks (once) and `.nemesis/`. Relay the output verbatim. |
| `/acos-nemesis-protocol on "<goal>" [--prd <file>]` | If the goal is missing or vague, ask DERIVE or PROVIDE exactly as `acos-oracle-protocol` does (same `looksVague` rule). Then `$N on "<goal>" …`. Relay verbatim. Oracle may already be on; Nemesis never changes Oracle's state. |
| `/acos-nemesis-protocol off` | `$N off` |
| `/acos-nemesis-protocol status` | `$N status` — show whole. |
| anything else / no argument | `$N status`, then the short menu: on / off / enroll / task start / report. |

## The loop as the hooks enforce it (every step has an artifact; none relies on memory)

1. **Start a task** — `$N task start <id> --title "…" [--prd-refs R-001,R-002]`.
2. **S0 threat sketch** — write `.nemesis/tasks/<id>/threat-sketch.yaml` from
   `templates/threat-sketch.yaml`, then `$N task sketch <id> <file>`. The CLI validates it
   (six STRIDE lines, data classes, two-sentence abuse case, valid checklist ids).
3. **S1 design review** — `templates/design-review.yaml` → `$N task design <id> <file>`. The
   daemon then authors and seals the task's acceptance holdout (S7) from the PRD and S1; the
   writer never sees it (a redesign seals a new version).
   Every checklist not ticked in the sketch needs `$N task checklist <id> <3.x> na "<why>"`.
   **Until S0 and S1 exist as files, the PreToolUse hook denies every in-scope edit** and
   names the command to run.
4. **S2, every edit** — the PreToolUse hook computes the candidate file, sends diff + file +
   the contract (goal, PRD excerpt, S0, S1, the twelve questions, the selected checklists,
   open issues and prior verdicts for the file — never the chat) to the Nemesis daemon. Opus
   reads callers/tests/policies (read-only) and answers JSON. A finding = deny; the reason
   carries each finding and the current rung instruction. Fixes are edits and are judged too.
5. **S3, every change** — the PostToolUse hook runs the whole-repo scan (type-check, lint,
   tests, secrets, dependency audit, RLS/security_invoker policy check, sensitive-data check).
   `skipped` is never clean. Failures open issues and come back as a block. Files changed by
   Bash are diffed against the pre-command snapshot and judged after the fact.
6. **S4, the ladder** — issues climb only when the JUDGE says stuck (no counter): rewrite →
   root cause → fresh-context agent (`Task(developer)` with the issue file only) →
   `$N second-opinion <id>` → `$N task redesign …` → `$N revert <id>` (last green snapshot,
   issue stays open) → task-end retry from rung 3 → goal-end product report. Print the
   current instruction with `$N ladder <id>`.
   **The advice is ENFORCED, not merely printed (D-56, R-074).** After three consecutive
   denials on one file in which the judge said STUCK and asked for rung 2 or higher, the next
   edit to THAT file is refused BEFORE a judge call is spent, quoting the rung instruction and
   the judge's reason. Every other file stays editable — rung 2's root cause usually lives in
   one of them. The way out is `$N rung ack <id> --action <root-cause|fresh-agent|second-opinion|redesign|revert> --why "<what you changed>"`,
   capped at two per file until a judged-clean edit lands there; after that only `$N task
   redesign` (rung 5) and `$N revert` (rung 6) clear it. `$N rung brakes` shows what is held.
7. **Close an issue** — `$N issue repro <id> <testFile> <testName>` then `$N issue close <id>`.
   Close succeeds only when the reproduction test passes, the suite passes, the last two
   scans are clean, and the independent review (`$N review <id>` or task end) confirms.
8. **Task end** — `$N task end <id>`: last two scans clean (S4) → independent adversarial
   review by the daemon (S6) → holdout reveal in QA context (S7) → S5 record assembled from
   the ledger → task closes, or every open issue goes to rung 7 → 3 with its full history.
9. **Goal end** — write `.nemesis/records/release-checklist.json` (backups restore tested,
   monitoring/alerts, incident contact, secrets rotated if exposed, branch protection — each
   `done` or `n/a` + note), then `$N release-gate` (S8). If issues are still open, the gate
   splits them by OWNER (amended 2026-11-02): a finding goes to rung 8 only if it passes all
   three admission tests, and everything else is named as the machine's own work with the
   command that clears it. Only when the gate is OPEN does the Stop hook accept
   `AUTOPILOT_GOAL_COMPLETE`.
10. **Report for Zee** — `$N report` (ledger) or `$N report --product` (rung 8). The product
    report is GROUPED by the feature he could drop — one question per group, not one section
    per finding — and machine work is counted, never listed. Plain language, local HTML, no
    code. Record his pick for a whole group with
    `$N issue decide <id>[,<id>...] --choice drop|change|accept --owner <who>`.

## Enforcement (what the hooks and CLI check — mirrors `prd/PRD.md` §5)

| Step | Artifact | Enforced by | Relies on the model for |
|---|---|---|---|
| S0 | `tasks/<id>/threat-sketch.yaml` + S0 hash | PreToolUse denies edits until present; CLI validates | composing it honestly (the judge checks it against the diff) |
| S1 | `tasks/<id>/design-review.yaml` + S1 hash | same | composing the design |
| S2 | verdict record + S2 entry per edit | PreToolUse judge; deny blocks the write | nothing |
| S3 | `records/scans/<n>.json` + S3 entry | PostToolUse runs it; block on failure | nothing |
| S4 | issue files, rungs | Stop hook blocks while open; judge advances rungs | writing the fix |
| S5 | `records/<task>/check-record.yaml` | `task end` assembles; refuses if incomplete | nothing |
| S6 | `records/<task>/review-*.json` | `task end` / `review` run the daemon attacker | nothing |
| S7 | `holdout/<task>/manifest.json` + reveal | daemon authors+seals at `task design` (new version on `task redesign`); `task end` reveals | nothing |
| S8 | `records/release-gate.json` | `release-gate`; Stop hook requires it | writing n/a reasons |
| PFC a,c,d,e | inside each verdict + scans | PreToolUse judge + PostToolUse scan | nothing |
| PFC b | the ladder | Stop hook + judge | writing the fix; spawning the rung-3 agent |
| Watchdog | `FUN-` issues | Stop hook: transcript vs records | nothing |
| Stalled writer | product report, stop allowed | Stop hook after `stalled_turns` (default 6) no-tool turns | — |

Honest limits: composing S0/S1, writing fixes, spawning the rung-3 agent, and applying a
rung-4 patch are the model's work. Everything else is a file a hook checks.

## Hard rules

- **Never claim what the CLI did not print.** Relay `DENIED` / `clean` / `CLOSED` / `refused` lines verbatim.
- **No bypass exists.** The only skips are the judge child's token and `test_mode` (never written by `enroll`).
- **Nemesis never stands down for the eternity window** (D-17); eternity's own files are out of scope.
- **Two judges, two charters.** Oracle leans allow (harm). Nemesis leans deny (rulebook). Never merge them.
- **Zee is never asked to fix code.** Rung 8 asks for a product decision only.
- **ONLY A FUNCTIONALITY OR DESIGN CHANGE REACHES ZEE (D-50, R-066..R-068, 2026-11-04).** This is
  the test that actually holds the door, because it is about the KIND of question and not the
  count. Zee: *"Now it is 23, if I do a large enough job, it could become 230… Other than when it
  comes to design, that I can see with my own eyes, no coding decision should come to me unless I
  specifically want some change be made… It is the systems job to code to ensure the functionality
  and the security."*
  - **A SECURITY finding (lens PSC) can never be his, at any rung.** His answer covers all of them
    — no lapse, in any way — so none has a question left in it. It cannot be decided and cannot
    even be CLAIMED as his. The decide refusal comes BEFORE the rung check, because being a
    security finding is permanent while "not at rung 8 yet" is a position.
  - **Everything else needs a confirmed claim:** `$N issue product-question <id> --kind
    <functionality|design> --why "<why no code change can clear it>"`, agreed by a reader that
    never spoke to the claiming judge. That reader is told to DEFEND HIS ATTENTION; its default
    answer is "machine work"; and a bug, a crash, a wrong result, two documents disagreeing, a
    stale count or date, a comment that contradicts its code, or any uncertainty are all machine
    work however often they recur. **A refused claim is permanent.** An exhausted ladder is no
    longer a reason to ask him.
  - **"Accept the risk with an owner" is DELETED.** It existed only so he could sign off a lapse he
    has ruled he will never sign off. Rung 8 offers TWO choices: drop the feature, or change the
    requirement. Measured before removing it: of 88 decisions ever recorded on the live ledger, 88
    were `drop` and 0 were `accept`. The release gate is strictly TIGHTER as a result — a secret
    finding used to be acceptable, which took it off the open list; now it must actually be fixed,
    and R-034 still blocks the release until a rotation is recorded.
  - **The KIND test runs LAST**, after the five below, so a refusal always names the machine's next
    MOVE instead of a claim to make. Putting it first made every refusal read "claim it if you
    think it is Zee's", which is the wrong instruction when it is not.
  - **Measured on website-design-okoa/v2:** of 464 open findings, **0** reach Zee — 74 Nemesis's
    own paperwork, 46 security, 221 with nothing yet tried, 102 still owing a rung, and 21 that
    cleared every other gate and are refused precisely because they are not functionality or
    design questions. Of the 23 that the previous amendment had left, 2 were security and about
    twelve were one document contradicting another; **none was a functionality or design change.**

- **A finding reaches Zee only through THREE admission tests (D-42/D-43/D-44, amended
  2026-11-02).** Rung 8 used to be a blanket sweep: `release-gate` moved every open finding
  there and handed Zee the lot. On website-design-okoa/v2 that was 356 findings — 104 of them
  about Nemesis's own paperwork, 0 ever moved up a rung by the judge, 83 jumped from rung 1 to
  Zee in one hop — and his answer was *"I don't even know what i need to decide."* A finding
  is now admitted only when:
  1. **it is in the PRODUCT lane** (`lib/lane.ts` `laneOf`): a stale sketch, a missing verdict
     or a skipped check is Nemesis's OWN record and the machine repairs it. The place the judge
     POINTS AT decides — a `where` under `.nemesis/` is a record even when the finding also
     names a shipping file, and a `where` on a shipping file stays product even when it also
     names a record. Checklist `3.4` (secrets) is the one rule that is product lane wherever
     its files live, because a hard-coded credential inside `.nemesis/` is a real credential.
     `record` never drops a finding: it stays OPEN and the gate stays SHUT, so the lane decides
     WHO, never WHETHER;
  2. **the question is answerable** (`admission`): the task is on record with a title (the
     FEATURE he could drop) and at least one `prd_refs` entry (the REQUIREMENT he could
     change). Missing refs are machine work, so they have a command:
     `$N task prd-refs <task> "<R-001,R-002>"`;
  3. **the machine has spent its own ladder** (`ladderExhausted`): rungs **2, 3, 4, 5 and 6**
     — every rung the machine owns — each have a REAL attempt, or a judge ruling naming why
     they cannot apply: `$N ladder <id> --na <n> --why "<reason>"`. `--why` is mandatory and a
     position rung (7, 8) can never be ruled out this way. Three rungs the CLI spends itself
     count once they record a result: rung 4 (`$N second-opinion <id>`, including the honest
     `unavailable` when no external model is configured), rung 5 (`$N task redesign`) and
     rung 6 (`$N revert`).
     **Being PARKED at a rung is not an attempt at it.** Task end parks an open finding at
     rung 3 and writes `7 → 3`; that is a position, not work.
     **An N/A ruling moves along the ladder, it never shortens it.** Measured on
     website-design-okoa/v2 on 2026-11-02: with only rungs 2, 3 and 4 required, the machine
     ruled rung 2 out 345 times and rung 3 out 151 times — with 123 distinct, well-argued
     reasons — and 356 findings reached Zee anyway, while its own reasons named rung 5 as the
     route it should have taken.
  **THREE CHECKS AND BALANCES ON THAT ESCAPE (D-49, R-061..R-065, 2026-11-04).** The escape
  itself had no check on it, and it became the new front door: 496 rulings, 123 distinct
  reasons, reviewed by nobody, 356 findings through anyway, 83 of them from rung 1 in one hop.
  Zee: *"Just using the judgement is not enough, it has to be best judgement with checks and
  balances. The codes writed needs to be invincible."* So:
  - **THE FLOOR.** At least ONE required rung must be spent on REAL WORK — an attempt the judge
    saw, a second opinion genuinely `received`, a redesign, or a revert. An all-N/A ladder is not
    an exhausted ladder, and neither is one cleared by a recorded NON-EVENT. Measured
    2026-11-04: an `unavailable` second opinion used to count, and since that project has no
    external model configured (**400** of 447 open carry `unavailable`, **338** of the 376 in the
    product lane, and **0** anywhere carry `received`) that single non-event was the whole ladder
    for 243 of the 266 product-lane findings being admitted. Counting real work only took
    **admitted to Zee from 266 to 23**. The OWED list still accepts `unavailable`, so rung 4
    never deadlocks; only the floor is strict.
    **Which rung you can actually PAY depends on the task in hand.** Rungs 2 and 3 are paid by an
    edit being re-judged, and `recordFindings` appends an attempt only when the finding's task
    matches the task being worked — so on a finding belonging to another task (117 of 247
    floor-refused on the measured ledger) a fresh agent can do real work and nothing records it.
    No deadlock: `$N task redesign <task> <issue> <file>` (rung 5) and `$N revert <issue>`
    (rung 6) take the target explicitly with no rung gate and no current-task gate, so they are
    payable on anything. The refusal names the creditable route and says how to make rung 3
    payable. **And an attempt records the rung the WORK was done at, never a position:**
    `workRungFor` maps a finding parked at 7 or 8 to rung 3, per rung 7's own "retry from rung 3"
    instruction, because an attempt stamped 8 paid for no required rung at all.
  - **INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION.** `$N ladder <id> --na <n> --why` writes `rung-na-proposed`,
    which counts for nothing. Only `rung-na-confirmed` counts, written after a second reader
    that never saw the first judge's reasoning agrees the rung is IMPOSSIBLE rather than merely
    unpromising. That reader is told its job is to DEFEND the ladder, so its default answer is
    that a rung can be attempted. No confirmation obtainable → the ruling is recorded and
    uncounted, and the command exits non-zero.
  - **A RECURRING REASON BECOMES A POLICY.** A reason already used on `na_reason_cap` findings
    (default 3) is refused; it must be written once as a standing rule instead. Counting is
    over the NORMALIZED reason, so re-wording does not reset it.

- **KNOWN LIMIT: `validateDesign` is a field check, not a parser.** Raised by the `v2-ee` session
  2026-11-04 and verified: there is NO YAML parser anywhere in `scripts/`, and every
  `design-review*.yaml` on that project fails `Bun.YAML.parse` with `Unexpected token` — including
  the accepted R-001, R-045, R-052 and R-056 designs. The files carry a `.yaml` extension but are
  read as a line-oriented format by regex. So **"validateDesign passed" is not evidence that a
  design file is well-formed YAML**, and it never checked the SUBSTANCE of a rule either — only
  that seven named fields are present, that `typed_errors` names `PERMISSION_DENIED` and
  `NOT_FOUND`, that `logging.never_log` is non-empty, that `authorization_matrix` lists an
  `- action:` row, and that no `<…>` placeholder survives.
  **What it DOES still guarantee, measured:** a commented-out field does NOT satisfy it — the key
  regex is anchored `^\s*key\s*:`, so `# data_flow:` is refused. `v2-ee` reported that a `#`
  comment "satisfies every check it makes"; that is not so, and it was checked by running
  `validateDesign` over a real design and over the same text fully commented out (0 problems vs
  8). Stripping comments before the check would change the verdict on NONE of the 30 design files
  on that project, so the regexes are reading real fields today.
  **Deliberately NOT fixed:** adding a hard YAML gate would refuse all 30 of those files at once,
  including live accepted designs, which would be a destructive change dressed as a correction.
  The real design review is the JUDGE reading the file whole (`lib/judge.ts` passes
  `fs.readFileSync(p, "utf8")`), not this shape check.

- **A RE-AUTHOR NEVER LOSES THE OLD COMMITMENT (D-51, R-069).** `$N task holdout <id> --new` and
  `$N task redesign` both go through `reauthorHoldout`, which puts the previous `manifest.json`
  back if authoring refuses — `authorTaskHoldout` never throws, it RETURNS a refusal string, so
  spending the manifest first used to leave a task with a pack and no manifest. Same defect as
  D-47, one layer up. **When a defect is "destructive step before fallible step", fix every
  caller, not just the layer where it surfaced.**

- **A RESTORE THAT ONLY RUNS IN-PROCESS DOES NOT COVER A KILL (D-53, R-071).** D-51's restore sits
  in a `catch` and a post-return check, so a SIGKILL reaches neither and leaves a pack with no
  commitment and no record. `reauthorHoldout` now writes `reauthor.inflight.json`, naming the exact
  `.spent-<ms>.json` to put back, BEFORE the rename; `recoverInterruptedReauthor` puts it back on
  the next command and in `nemesis status`. **The trap:** restoring BEFORE the kill is destructive —
  the sealer throws when a manifest exists (`acos-holdout-test/scripts/holdout.ts:106`) and nemesis
  passes no `--force`, so a manifest put back under a living author turns a success into a refusal.
  **Kill first, then restore**; spent files are never deleted. Recovery only ever acts when there is
  no `manifest.json` and the marker's pid is dead, so it cannot overwrite a live commitment. It
  prevents the next occurrence and **cannot repair a task killed before any marker existed** — a
  commitment is never guessed.

- **A COUNT IS NOT AN IDENTITY (D-52, R-070).** `$N task redesign` keeps the outgoing design as
  `design-review.v<N>.yaml`, and `N` is the HIGHEST number already used plus one — never the count
  of matching files. Counting collides the instant anything is removed or renamed (v1..v15 present,
  delete v3, and count+1 is 15, which exists), and `copyFileSync` overwrites a stored prior design
  without complaint. That design is a permanent record: `steps.json` pins the sha256 of the S1
  artifact actually installed. The copy now REFUSES if the target name exists, and a design whose
  opening line hand-types a version draws a NOTE, because a derived name and a typed header have
  nothing tying them together — six of sixteen archived R-019 designs name the wrong version, and
  **an already-archived design is never edited to fix it.** Third instance of this substitution:
  D-44/D-49 counted findings for the admission gate, D-50 replaced the count with the KIND.

- **A CONDITION CHECKED ONCE AND RECORDED IS A CLAIM, NOT A GUARANTEE (D-59, R-077).** Closure
  criterion (1) is a reproduction test that exists, is declared, is unskipped and passed. It was
  verified on the day of the close and never again, because every live `runScan` caller passes
  `listIssues(root, true)` — open findings only. So a closed finding's proof fell out of every
  check in the system while `lib/report.ts` kept saying it "was Closed after the reproduction test
  passed", in the present tense. Measured on website-design-okoa/v2: **177 closed findings rest on
  13 proof files, and `_build/headers.test.ts` alone carries 34** — deleting that one file would
  un-prove 34 closed findings and the release gate would have opened anyway. `cmdReleaseGate` now
  re-asks the question of every closed finding, refusing with the id, the close date and the
  reason, and the rule is SHARED (`reproEvidence`) rather than copied, so the gate and the scan
  cannot drift. It sits at the gate, not in the scan: a proof cannot rot between two turns of one
  session, it rots between a close in October and a release in November. **Fourth member of one
  family: D-52 a count for an identity, D-53 state left behind for a run record, D-57 an absent
  field for a zero, D-59 a past verification for a present fact.**

- **A GATE THAT CANNOT PASS IS NOT STRICT, IT IS A DEADLOCK (D-58, R-076).** `runReview` returned
  `confirmed: !!r.confirmed_fixed && findings.length === 0`. The reviewer reads the WHOLE task diff
  under the twelve questions and the PFC lens, so on any real ledger it finds something somewhere —
  and that something vetoed the focus verdict it had just given. Measured on website-design-okoa/v2:
  ten `nemesis review` runs, **zero confirmed**; **87 of 156** findings created that day (56%) were
  opened BY a review; and **117 of 117** closes the ledger ever had required a confirmed review. So
  the only action that could shrink the pile was the action that grew it fastest — D-55's multiplier
  arithmetic at the closing end of the pipe. SEC-10 had a passing reproduction and its third review
  said *"SEC-10's own complaints are resolved on disk"* while refusing over a one-line citation drift
  and a sentence it said itself was already filed as SEC-97 and FUN-951. **With a focus the verdict is
  the focus verdict**; other findings are filed, because they are real, but they do not veto a
  different finding's fix. Without a focus (task end) the review IS the whole task and the
  zero-findings rule stands. The charter now asks for `focus_fixed`, `focus_why` and `already_filed`,
  and says the separation HIGH UP instead of in one line under "your one goal: BREAK IT". **And the
  de-duplication key dropped the line number** — `where` is `file:line`, so a drifted line opened a
  new finding for a defect already on record; it keys on the FILE now and writes the move into the
  history. That is D-52 a second time: a line number is not an identity.
  **When a verdict about one thing is vetoed by facts about another thing, the gate measures the
  neighbourhood, not the subject.**

- **AN ABSENT FIELD AND A ZERO FIELD ARE THE SAME BYTES AND OPPOSITE FACTS (D-57, R-075).** All four
  Stop records now carry `streak` and `last_turn_tool_calls`. The release branch carried neither,
  and that is the one branch where "was the writer working or spinning?" is the question — reading
  the absence as a zero produced a confident, wrong diagnosis on 2026-11-09 (8 releases reported as
  turns with no tool calls, while `idle.json` held `last_turn_tool_calls: 4` and every blocked
  record in the window carried `streak: 0`). **When a record omits a field on one branch only, that
  branch will be misdiagnosed, and the first reader to do it will be confident.** Separately, the
  per-turn progress write passed three fields to `writeIdle`, which is a FULL overwrite, so
  `last_block_sha`, `latched_sha`, `same_block_streak` and `block_chain` were deleted from disk every
  turn; the later branches wrote them back from memory, which hid it, but the `!d.open` early return
  does not, so one such turn wiped the host-chain count and D-46's report latch. It now spreads
  `...idle`. That is the THIRD door into D-54 — a write that drops a field something else is still
  counting — and it is fixed at the source this time, not one branch at a time.

- **A RECOMMENDATION THAT NOTHING REFUSES IS A COMMENT (D-56, R-074).** `applyAssessment` raised
  a finding's rung whenever the judge said the writer was stuck, and that was all it did: write a
  label. Measured on website-design-okoa/v2 over 553 judged edits — **227 (41%) were attempted
  after the judge had already flagged `stuck`, and 9 passed.** A 4% pass rate against 61% when not
  stuck; 124 of the 227 on ONE file; the judge asked for rung 2 166 times and rung 5 48 times
  while the writer kept editing the same file. At 2.0 findings per denial those ~218 doomed edits
  manufactured ~436 findings, about 44% of every finding the project ever had. The diagnosis was
  right all along and walking past it cost nothing. **The brake refuses BEFORE the judge call**,
  because the judge call is most of the cost and a brake that still pays for the verdict saves
  nothing. **The ack exists because rung 3 is "a fresh agent takes over" and a fresh agent edits
  the SAME file through the same tool** — nothing in the edit distinguishes it, so the ladder step
  has to be said out loud and recorded, and the next judge reads the claim. **The ack is capped at
  two per file** because an unlimited one is tape: say the words, edit the same way, repeat. It
  cannot deadlock — `rung ack`, `task redesign` and `revert` all run through the CLI, which this
  hook allows unjudged, and each takes its target explicitly with no rung gate. This is D-49 seen
  from the other side: there, an escape with no check on it became the front door; here, an
  instruction with no check on it was ignored.

- **POLICIES — standing rules the machine applies to itself (D-49).** A judgement made three
  times is a standing rule, and a standing rule belongs in one reviewed, vetoable record
  rather than a 497th history line nobody will audit.
  ```
  $N policy template                     # print a draft to fill in
  $N policy propose <draft.json>         # refused unless rule, why, EVIDENCE and both test cases are present
  $N policy review <POL-n>               # independent reader; activates ONLY on approval
  $N policy apply <issue> | --all         # move matching findings to the rung their class takes
  $N policy list                          # what the machine has decided for itself, and how often it fired
  $N policy veto <POL-n> --why "<reason>" # Zee's veto: stops it AND walks back every finding it moved
  ```
  **THE INVINCIBILITY LINE.** A policy may choose only among rungs **2-6**, the ones the
  machine owns. It can never close, decide, drop, accept or rule a finding N/A — and the
  `Policy` type has no field that could express any of those, so the guarantee is structural,
  not a check that can be forgotten. D-11's four closure criteria still hold for every finding,
  every time: a reproduction test that passes, a green suite, two clean scans, an independent
  reviewer. **A policy makes the machine faster at spending its own options. It can never make
  the gate laxer.**
  A draft is also refused when its matcher is too NARROW to catch the case it says motivated it,
  or too WIDE — catching the near-miss the policy itself declares it must not. That second check
  exists because my own lane fix on 2026-11-02 was one step too wide and reclassified 17
  findings it was never meant to touch.

  `issue decide` re-runs all three, so a finding parked at rung 8 before the gate existed is
  still not decidable. Nothing is weakened: an unadmitted finding stays OPEN and the release
  gate stays SHUT — it is named as the machine's work rather than as Zee's question.
- **The Stop hook releases before Claude Code overrides it (D-46).** A blocking Stop hook that
  the host has to override loses control of its own contract and ends the turn with no record
  of why; that happened twice (2026-11-01T08:00, 2026-11-02T06:16, "blocked the turn from
  ending 9 consecutive times"). The hook therefore reads `stop_hook_active` and keeps two
  counters in `.nemesis/state/idle.json`: identical directives repeated, and consecutive
  blocks in one Stop chain. Either exceeding `max_identical_blocks` (default 6, below the
  host's 8) releases the turn with an honest note — the goal is NOT complete, the ledger is
  NOT cleared, and here is what is owed and by whom. The chain counter is the one that
  matters: the watchdog opened a new finding almost every turn, so the directive changed every
  turn and a text-keyed counter would have reset each time. The stalled escape (R-054) now
  LATCHES on the ledger's fingerprint instead of re-arming — it had written 16 duplicate
  reports totalling 8.9 MB.
- **Hook text is BUDGETED, and the block is the enforcement — not the prose.** The Stop and
  UserPromptSubmit hooks name the worst-severity issues, state the true open count, say how
  many they did not name and at what severities, and point at `nemesis issue list --open`.
  They never enumerate the ledger: that text lands in the writer's conversation every turn,
  and on 2026-11-01 an unbounded Stop reason over 436 open issues measured 336,897 chars
  (~84,224 tokens) per turn and compacted a 500,000-token context 73 times. Limits live in
  `.nemesis/config.json` (`stop_issue_detail`, `stop_directive_max_chars`,
  `prompt_issue_detail`, `prompt_context_max_chars`); the reasoning is in `scripts/lib/digest.ts`.
- **Sealing a holdout pack never destroys the pack it replaces (D-47 / R-059).** The new pack is
  written to `pack.staging-<ts>/`, sealed FROM there, and moved into place only when the seal
  succeeds; a refusal deletes the staging copy and leaves the previously sealed pack and its
  manifest byte-for-byte as they were. Measured in one project's own `holdout.jsonl`: five
  `seal-failed` events (one bad `status` value, four "manifest already exists"), and on every one
  the new files had already overwritten the sealed pack while the manifest still committed to the
  old hashes. Nemesis also pre-checks that every expectation carries the key its grade mode is
  read for — `exit` for `exit-code`, `stdout` for `stdout-json`/`stdout-text` — which is a second
  line of defence, not the guarantee: `acos-holdout-test/.../lib/pack.ts` has refused ungradable
  and unknown-key packs at seal time since 2026-10-31. A reveal with NO verdict is reported as a
  PACK defect naming the missing keys, printing only case ids and key names, never an expected
  value.
- **A reproduction test is credited only on positive evidence (D-48 / R-060).** "Nothing said it
  failed" is not evidence that anything ran. The named test must be DECLARED in the file the
  reproduction points at, not skipped or todo, with the suite green; every refusal records its
  cause and `closureCheck` prints it beside criterion (1). The evidence is read from the FILE, not
  the suite output, because `bun test` prints only `1 pass` / `0 fail` on success and names a test
  only when it fails — measured, so demanding a pass marker would have blocked every project
  using it. The hole it closes: a name present nowhere produced no failure line, so it was
  credited, and one finding closed on a reproduction pointing at an unrelated test.
- **Zero open at every severity.** There is no defer command; Low blocks the release gate.

